Z||000007||Formula||07-17-2000||04:20 PM||||if you calculate it....<BR>mustang  4200/190=22.1053 Lbs/horsepower<BR>fiero 2800/135=20.7 Lbs/Horsepower<P>The fiero actually has a better horsepower to weight ratio then the mustang.<P>now if I got my 3.4 that I've been drooling over....<P>3.4 Fiero 2800/210=13.3 Lbs/Horsepower<BR>.......................................<P>now in terms of tq...<P>Mustang 4200/220= 19.09 lbs/ft lbs<BR>Fiero 2800/150= 18.6667 lbs/ft lbs<P>This is assuming that the fiero is in good running condition and that it is producing 150 ft lbs of tq....<P>but yet again the fiero beats the 2000 Mustang in performance specs according to the 4200 gross weight that the mustang has on the door!!!!  (I think it SHULD be lighter than that though)<P>------------------<BR>FORMULA......<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stripes1formula.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||07-17-2000||04:23 PM||||The Ford 3.8 V6 is a limp-peter motor.  They all sound like crap and I know three people personally who have experienced blown head gaskets with Ford 3.8's, including a guy here at work with a 95 Mustang which blew a gasket at 65K miles.<P>All of which made it even harder to take when I got dusted by a black V6 Mustang last week.  He had these cheesy personalized plates and that stupid chrome piping around his doors and over his wheel wells.  I suppose I can take solace in the fact that his Mustang will have gone through 2 or 3 head gaskets by the time it has 112K miles like my Duke.||||reg||8
